	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Jeni uses your camera to scan your meals and, if you use Body Vision, to take your private progress scans. Meal photos go to our private vision service for calorie and protein estimates — never used to train models. Progress scans are processed on your iPhone and never leave it unless you turn on backup. Small copies stay with your journal on your phone and in your private cloud space so it survives a new phone; you can turn photo keeping off in settings.</string>
	<key>NSHealthShareUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Jeni reads a few quiet signals from Apple Health — steps, workouts, walking distance, active energy, sleep, weigh-ins, resting heart rate, recovery (heart-rate variability), cycle timing, and body composition when your scale saves it — so your trend fills in without you typing a thing. These signals shape only your own plan and jeni's notes to you. Imported weigh-ins sync to your private Jeni account; nothing is sold, and nothing is shared unless you explicitly choose to share it.</string>
	<key>NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription</key>
	<string>If you turn on the Apple Health write toggle in Jeni's food settings, your logged meal calories are saved to Apple Health as Dietary Energy. Off by default. You can change this anytime.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Jeni saves your shared moments (food plates, becoming snapshots, lesson quotes) to your Photos so you can post them to TikTok or Instagram later. Nothing is saved without you tapping save.</string>
	<key>NSSupportsLiveActivities</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSUserTrackingUsageDescription</key>
	<string>This lets us learn how you found us so Jeni can keep getting better for women like you. We never sell your data and you can change this anytime in Settings.</string>
	<!-- Meta (Facebook) SDK 18.1.1 — install/purchase attribution for
	     Meta ad campaigns. Key names read from the shipped FBSDKCoreKit
	     binary of this exact version, not from memory. -->
	<!-- OFF DELIBERATELY. RevenueCat forwards purchases to Meta
	     server-side; leaving Meta's own StoreKit observer on would
	     count every purchase twice (FBSDKAppEvents.m:1001 gates that
	     observer on this flag). Also set at runtime in
	     MetaAttributionService — see its header for the full note,
	     including the server-side value that outranks both. -->
	<key>FacebookAutoLogAppEventsEnabled</key>
	<false/>
